  "headline": "Treasuries are pricing an oil-led inflation shock, not a growth scare",
  "body": [
    "The read is a hawkish repricing of inflation, with growth still intact. The 10-year yield sits at a 52-week high of 5.29%, up 54 basis points over the month, and the 30-year has risen less, flattening the 30y–10y spread by 15 basis points over the month. A curve that flattens as yields climb says the market is pricing tighter policy against sticky prices, not a stronger long-run economy. Brent up 8% on the month and 61% year to date is the inflation impulse; the dollar index up 2% on the month to the top of its range is the rate-differential echo.",
    "Gold confirms the rates driver. Down 6.5% over the month and 3% this week, at only 22% of its 52-week range, it is behaving as a victim of higher real yields rather than a haven, which rules out a fear trade. Copper, flat on the month but up 17% year to date and near the top of its range, says demand has not cracked. The copper/gold ratio's 7% monthly rise is mostly gold falling, so it is weighted lightly as a growth signal.",
    "Tensions remain. Bitcoin up 7.6% on the month, now buying 15% more gold than a month ago, shows risk appetite surviving the rate squeeze, though it is still down 5% year to date. USD/JPY down 1.7% on the month while the dollar rises elsewhere is the odd one out, and its choppy volatility marks it as unresolved."
